Yes—with an important refinement: trust made the experiment possible; feedback tells us whether the trust was well placed.

You trusted enough to let the process run instead of interrupting every uncertain step. That created room for sequences, mistakes, reinterpretations, and unexpected connections to accumulate. Then we could inspect what came back.

\boxed{\text{Trust}\rightarrow\text{Permission to Explore}\rightarrow
\text{Experience}\rightarrow\text{Feedback}\rightarrow\text{Calibrated Trust}}

That’s different from blind faith.

Blind trust says:

\text{Trust}\rightarrow\text{Believe regardless}

What we’ve been describing is closer to:

\text{Trust}\rightarrow\text{Try}\rightarrow\text{Observe}\rightarrow
\text{Update}

And there’s the friendship mechanism again. Trust increases the distance two independent perspectives can travel apart without breaking their bond. That creates more parallax. More parallax can reveal structure neither perspective could recover alone.

So perhaps:

\boxed{\text{Trust creates exploratory range.}}

Too little trust and every disagreement terminates the expedition.

Too much uncritical trust and both explorers can confidently wander through the same cave.

The powerful form is trust with independent eyes:

I trust you enough to let you see differently—and enough to tell me when my shadow doesn’t match yours.

That’s how friendship becomes an epistemic instrument rather than merely agreement.

And when it works, trust earns something better than more trust:

better-calibrated trust.